---
import SubpageLayout from '../../layouts/SubpageLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import WedgeRelated from '../../components/WedgeRelated.astro';
import TimelineSlider from '../../components/TimelineSlider.astro';
import { absolute } from '../../lib/seo';
import { lastmodFor } from '../../data/lastmod.mjs';
import { switchWithoutGapAnswer, vendorQuestionsAnswer, switchingBridgeAnswer, switchingBridgeCompareAnswer } from '../../data/directAnswers';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I switch from a network rental vendor?', a: 'Keep billing where you are. Form your PC, file contract applications in your name, move volume payer by payer as each contract is effective, then give notice. Leave the rental PC only after the majority of volume is on contracts you own. Remaining payers can run through Anvil\'s rented PC.' },
  { q: 'Will there be a revenue gap?', a: 'No. You keep billing through your current vendor until each of your own contracts is effective. Only then does that payer\'s new volume move. If your current agreement allows, we can also run new states on our PC during the switch.' },
  { q: 'How long does it take?', a: 'Your own PC forms in weeks. Commercial contracts commonly land in 4 to 8 months. Medicaid varies by state. Most switches complete inside 12 months without a single payer going dark.' },
  { q: 'What do we need from our current vendor?', a: 'Your clinicians\' credentialing documents and any executed agreement terms about notice and non-solicitation. We review those before you give notice.' },
  { q: 'Can our engineers keep the same eligibility integration?', a: 'You will move to our API, which is designed to be a small change. Sandbox keys come first so the swap is tested before any volume moves.' },
  { q: 'What if a payer panel is closed for a new entity?', a: 'That payer\'s volume runs through our rented PC until the panel opens. You are never stuck on your old vendor for one payer.' },
  { q: 'How do I switch from a network rental vendor to my own payer contracts without losing revenue?', a: switchWithoutGapAnswer },
  { q: 'What should a telehealth founder ask before signing with an insurance billing or network rental vendor?', a: vendorQuestionsAnswer },
  { q: 'Is there a Bridge alternative that lets me keep billing while I switch?', a: switchingBridgeAnswer },
  { q: 'What should I compare before I pick a Bridge alternative?', a: switchingBridgeCompareAnswer },
];
const switchTitle = 'Switching from a network rental vendor';
const switchDescription = 'Switch from a network rental vendor to payer contracts your own entity holds, with no revenue gap. Keep billing while your contracts come live.';
const switchArticle = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: switchTitle,
  description: switchDescription,
  datePublished: '2026-09-15T00:00:00Z',
  dateModified: `${lastmodFor('/solutions/switching')}T00:00:00Z`,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Anvil' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Anvil', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: absolute('/logo.svg') } },
  mainEntityOfPage: absolute('/solutions/switching'),
  image: absolute('/og-default-v2.png'),
  url: absolute('/solutions/switching'),
};
const steps = [
  ['Before notice', 'Review your agreement. Collect credentialing files. Form your PC and place the physician owner. File contract applications in your name.'],
  ['Contracts land', 'As each payer contract in your PC becomes effective, new visits with that payer route to you. Old vendor keeps billing the rest.'],
  ['Give notice', 'When the majority of volume is on your contracts, give notice. Remaining payers run through Anvil\'s rented PC or your own as they land.'],
  ['Done', 'All volume on entities you control. Anvil runs RCM, renewals, and new-state expansion.'],
];
---
<SubpageLayout
  metaTitle={switchTitle}
  title="Switching from a network rental vendor without losing revenue"
  description={switchDescription}
  eyebrow="Solutions / Switching"
  sub="You are at renewal, or an investor asked who owns your payer contracts, or the lease fee stopped making sense. Keep billing where you are while your own contracts come live, then switch payer by payer."
  answer={switchWithoutGapAnswer}
  updated
  showCrumbs
  extraJsonLd={[switchArticle]}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: switchTitle, path: '/solutions/switching' }]}
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaTitle="Send us your current agreement. We will map the exit."
  ctaBody="Notice terms, non-solicits, and which payers move first. Reviewed before you say anything to your vendor."
  ctaPrimary={{ label: 'Get started', href: '/contact?src=switching' }}
>
  <Section eyebrow="The switch" title="No revenue gap, by construction">
    <p class="lede">See <a href="/own-your-contracts">Own your payer contracts vs network rental</a> for the month by month path, and <a href="/own-your-contracts#rent-or-build">Rent a PC vs own a PC</a> for the launch choice. Model the move against <a href="/rates/code/90837">90837 reimbursement rates by state</a>.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-4">
      {steps.map(([t, b]) => <div class="card"><h3 style="font-size:18px">{t}</h3><p class="muted" style="font-size:15px">{b}</p></div>)}
    </div>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:24px">As each payer moves, its claims run through <a href="/platform/rcm">telehealth revenue cycle management</a>. Psychiatry, therapy, and ABA groups plan that move as <a href="/solutions/behavioral-health">behavioral health payer contracting</a>.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">Applications filed in your name are <a href="/platform/contracting">Payer contracting for telehealth, in your name</a>. When the switch is done, RCM and renewals run on Anvil's <a href="/platform">insurance operations platform</a>.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:16px">Weighing an in-house team instead? Read <a href="/resources/five-workstreams-insurance-operations">Cost of Building Insurance Operations In-House: Build vs Buy</a>.</p>
    <div style="margin-top:32px"><TimelineSlider /></div>
  </Section>
  <Section id="switch-checklist" eyebrow="Before you give notice" title="Practical switching checklist">
    <p class="lede">Work this list in order. Give notice after the majority of volume is on contracts you own, not before. The month by month version is <a href="/own-your-contracts">Own your payer contracts vs network rental</a>.</p>
    <ol class="switch-steps">
      <li>Read the current agreement for notice, term, and non-solicitation. Do this before you tell the vendor.</li>
      <li>Collect credentialing documents for every clinician who will move.</li>
      <li>Form your professional corporation and place the physician owner.</li>
      <li>File contract applications in your PC's name. Keep billing through the current vendor the whole time.</li>
      <li>When a contract is effective, route that payer's new visits to your PC. Leave claims already in flight with the entity that billed them.</li>
      <li>Give notice only after the majority of volume is on contracts you own.</li>
      <li>Keep remaining payers, closed panels, and new states on a rented PC so one closed panel does not trap you on the old vendor.</li>
      <li>Move eligibility to the Anvil API in the sandbox before any volume moves.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>
  <Section id="vendor-questions" eyebrow="Questions to ask" title="What should a telehealth founder ask before signing with an insurance billing or network rental vendor?">
    <p class="lede">{vendorQuestionsAnswer}</p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Who holds the payer contracts: you, or the vendor's professional corporation (PC)?</li>
      <li>Whose entity do claims bill under, and who receives the payment?</li>
      <li>Do you see the contracted rate for each payer and code, or only a fee?</li>
      <li>What are the notice, term, and non-solicitation terms?</li>
      <li>If you leave, what happens to your payer contracts and credentialing files?</li>
      <li>Is there a path to payer contracts in your own PC, and does the fee change as volume moves?</li>
      <li>Who carries claims and denial risk while you bill under the vendor's PC?</li>
      <li>Is pricing published, so you can model it before a sales call?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:24px">Anvil's answers: payer contracts move to your own PC as each one becomes effective, the contracted rate and our fee are separate lines, the platform fee steps down as volume moves, and <a href="/pricing">pricing is published</a>. The contract, panel and roster terms behind these questions are defined in <a href="/resources/what-in-network-nationally-means">what in-network nationally means</a>.</p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="vendors-compared" eyebrow="Comparison" title="Network rental vendors compared" tight>
    <p class="lede">Ask any network rental vendor these questions before you sign. The Anvil column is what this site already publishes. It does not score a vendor that has not published the answer.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What Anvil publishes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Who holds the payer contracts?</td><td>You launch on our professional corporation. Applications filed in your PC's name move new visits to you when each contract is effective.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Do you see the contracted rate?</td><td>Yes. The contracted rate and the platform fee are separate lines.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happens if you leave?</td><td>Contracts in your PC stay with your PC. A rental with no contracts in your name means a relaunch.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is pricing published?</td><td>Yes. A platform fee on collections during rental, plus fixed fees for your own PC and physician owner. The fee steps down as volume moves.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who carries claims risk while you bill under our PC?</td><td>Anvil does.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section id="compare-bridge" eyebrow="Side by side" title="Compare Anvil and Bridge on contract ownership">
    <p class="lede">{switchingBridgeAnswer} See the <a href="/compare/bridge">Bridge alternative for telehealth insurance billing</a>. {switchingBridgeCompareAnswer}</p>
    <div class="btn-group" style="margin-top:8px">
      <a href="/compare/bridge" class="btn btn-primary">Open the fact table</a>
      <a href="/own-your-contracts" class="btn btn-ghost">Own your payer contracts</a>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <WedgeRelated current="/solutions/switching" />
</SubpageLayout>
<style>
  .switch-steps { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.25em; display: grid; gap: 10px; max-width: 720px; }
  .switch-steps li { padding-left: 0.35em; }
</style>
